  <section id="sparkhire">
    <div class="section-num">§ 03</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Spark Hire — <em>format &amp; setup</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Spark Hire is asynchronous video. There is no live interviewer. The platform shows the question, gives you a "Think Time" countdown, then automatically starts recording. Knowing the mechanics is half the battle.</p>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>What every question shows you</h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Think Time</strong> — usually 30 seconds. Use it. Take a breath, jot 3 bullets on paper, then start.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Takes</strong> — often 1 to 3 retakes allowed. Don't burn them all on Question 1. Treat the first take as the real one unless something is genuinely broken.</p>
        <p><strong>Time Limit</strong> — typically 1 to 3 minutes per answer. Aim to finish around the 80% mark — never run out of time mid-sentence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>The technical setup</h3>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Browser</strong> — Google Chrome on a desktop computer. Not Safari, not Firefox.</p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 12px;"><strong>Audio</strong> — wear headphones with a mic. Cuts echo dramatically. Close every other app using camera or bandwidth (Zoom, WhatsApp Web, Teams).</p>
        <p><strong>Practice video</strong> — Spark Hire lets you record a practice clip before the real interview. Always do it. Check lighting on your face, not from behind.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout tip">
      <div class="callout-title">✦ The on-camera fundamentals candidates always miss</div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Look at the lens, not the screen.</strong> Put a sticky note arrow next to the webcam pointing to it.</li>
        <li><strong>Frame yourself at chest-up, with eyes one-third from the top.</strong> Don't film from below — laptop on books if needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Plain background, soft front light.</strong> A window in front of you (not behind) works better than any lamp.</li>
        <li><strong>Smile in the first 2 seconds, then settle.</strong> The AI screening tools and human reviewers both anchor on the opening.</li>
        <li><strong>Open with the question reframed.</strong> "Sure — when it comes to my biggest partnership win, the one that comes to mind is…" buys you 3 seconds and signals structure.</li>
        <li><strong>Close with a clear ending,</strong> not a trailing "…yeah, so, that's it I guess." End on the result.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠ Things that quietly disqualify candidates</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Reading visibly from notes off-screen (eyes darting left/right repeatedly).</li>
        <li>Going significantly over time on every question — signals poor self-regulation.</li>
        <li>Using all retakes on the first question, then having no retakes left when it matters.</li>
        <li>Background noise (kids, traffic, fan, TV). Record at a quiet time of day.</li>
        <li>Heavy filler words ("um," "like," "you know") — these are amplified on video.</li>
        <li>Speaking in only Egyptian Arabic when the job description is English. Default to English unless the question is asked in Arabic.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═════════════ 04 STAPLES ═════════════ -->
  <section id="staples">
    <div class="section-num">§ 04</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">The 9 <em>Spark Hire staples</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">These are Spark Hire's own published "most common" questions — the ones recruiters drag in by default when setting up a new interview template. Each comes with a model answer tailored to the Talabat partnerships role. Adapt, don't memorize.</p>

    <!-- Q1 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">01</div>
        <div class="q-text">Tell me about yourself.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">A 60–90 second snapshot that connects past work to <em>this</em> role. Not a resume read-through. They want to hear which thread of your career points at marketing partnerships.</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "I'm a marketing and partnerships professional with <strong>[X] years</strong> of experience, most recently leading <strong>[your most relevant function — partnerships, BD, account management, or marketing campaigns]</strong> at <strong>[company]</strong>. Over the last few years I've focused on building <strong>commercial partnerships that move both brand and revenue</strong> — most recently <strong>[one quick concrete example: e.g., a co-marketing deal with a bank, or an FMCG campaign that drove X% lift]</strong>. What pulls me toward this role at Talabat specifically is that partnerships here aren't a side function — they sit at the intersection of brand, growth, and category strategy, especially with how competitive Egypt's q-commerce space has become. Outside of work, <strong>[one short, humanizing line]</strong>."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Keep it to ~75 seconds. The last line — the personal one — is what makes reviewers remember you. Avoid: "I was born in…", chronological from university, or generic "I love working with people."</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q2 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">02</div>
        <div class="q-text">What interests you about this position?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Did you read the JD? Do you understand Talabat's stage in Egypt? Are you running <em>toward</em> this role or just running away from your current one?</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "Three things, specifically. First, the <strong>breadth of the partnerships portfolio</strong> — banking, FMCG, technology, entertainment, real estate — that's an unusually wide canvas, which means the role rewards curiosity and pattern-matching across industries, not just deep specialization in one. Second, the <strong>moment Talabat is in</strong> in Egypt — leading the market but actively defending it against Rabbit, Breadfast, Carrefour Now, and what's coming from Keeta. Partnerships are a real lever in that fight, not a vanity workstream. And third, the <strong>combination of strategy and execution</strong> — building the pipeline, negotiating the deal, then actually shipping the campaign and measuring it. That end-to-end ownership is exactly the shape of work I do best."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Mentioning competitors by name is high-leverage — it signals she's done her homework without sounding rehearsed. Don't say "I want to grow my career" — that's about her, not them.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q3 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">03</div>
        <div class="q-text">What are your strengths?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Can you self-assess accurately? Do your strengths actually match the role? They're listening for negotiation, structured thinking, and stakeholder management — not "I'm a fast learner."</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure — pick 2, illustrate each</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "Two stand out for this kind of role. The first is <strong>structured negotiation</strong> — I tend to enter every partnership conversation with a clear view of what both sides actually need, not just what they're asking for. That's let me close deals where the obvious commercial terms didn't work but a creative trade did — for example, <strong>[short example: trading premium placement for media co-investment, or a longer term for a better revenue share]</strong>. The second is <strong>stakeholder orchestration</strong> — partnership work falls apart at the handoffs between legal, finance, and marketing, so I've gotten good at running parallel workstreams without losing momentum. Concretely, <strong>[example of a deal you kept on track across multiple internal teams]</strong>."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Two strengths beats three or four. Each strength MUST come with a one-sentence proof. Avoid: "communication, teamwork, hard-working" — these are table-stakes, not strengths.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q4 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">04</div>
        <div class="q-text">How do you see yourself applying your strengths in this role?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Have you actually projected yourself into the day-to-day? Can you tie strengths back to the specific tasks in the JD?</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "The job description talks about three things that map directly onto how I work. <strong>Managing a partnership pipeline</strong> across multiple industries — that's where the structured negotiation strength applies, because each category (banking versus FMCG versus entertainment) has totally different decision-making rhythms and value drivers. <strong>End-to-end execution</strong> — that's where the stakeholder orchestration shows up: aligning Talabat's finance, legal, tech, and regional teams against an external partner's timeline. And <strong>performance analysis and reporting</strong> — that's where my Excel and presentation side comes in: I think of a partnership not as closed when the contract is signed, but when the post-mortem deck has been read by leadership and we've decided whether to renew."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Quoting back the JD's own language ("end-to-end execution", "pipeline") signals close reading. Do it once or twice — more than that sounds parroted.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q5 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">05</div>
        <div class="q-text">What are your weaknesses?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Are you self-aware? Can you talk honestly about growth without sounding either falsely modest ("I work too hard") or genuinely worrying ("I miss deadlines")?</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "Historically, my instinct has been to <strong>over-prepare before bringing a partnership opportunity to internal stakeholders</strong> — I'd want the full model, the full deck, the pricing scenarios all locked before looping in legal or finance. What I learned the hard way is that this delays deals and means feedback comes too late to shape them. So over the last <strong>[X months / project]</strong>, I've shifted to <strong>looping cross-functional partners in at the 60% mark</strong> rather than the 95% mark — sharing a one-page brief and a draft term sheet earlier. The trade-off is more meetings, but the deals move faster and the final contracts have fewer surprises."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label trap">Avoid these clichés</div>
        <p class="answer-text">"I'm a perfectionist" / "I work too hard" / "I care too much" — every recruiter has heard these 10,000 times. Pick a real, specific, but <em>recoverable</em> weakness and show the fix.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q6 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">06</div>
        <div class="q-text">Describe a challenge you've overcome in a previous role.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Can you tell a story with a clear shape — situation, obstacle, action, result? Did <em>you</em> do something specific, or did things "just work out"?</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer — SOAR framework</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "<strong>Situation</strong> — at <strong>[company]</strong>, we had a high-priority partnership with <strong>[partner]</strong> that was at risk of falling apart two weeks before launch because <strong>[specific friction: legal redlines stalled, partner's CMO changed, results below pilot expectations].</strong> <strong>Obstacle</strong> — the internal stakeholders were ready to walk away, but the deal represented <strong>[meaningful number: X% of quarterly partnerships revenue / first deal in that category].</strong> <strong>Action</strong> — I did three things in parallel: I rebuilt the business case with the actual pilot data to show where the underperformance was a tracking issue versus a real problem, I personally re-engaged the partner's new decision-maker with a revised proposal, and I negotiated a scaled-down launch with two clear gates before full investment. <strong>Result</strong> — we launched on a 4-week delay rather than killing the deal, hit <strong>[specific metric]</strong>, and that partnership is now <strong>[outcome — e.g., renewed, expanded, became case study]</strong>."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">The numbers and the "I" verbs are what matter. "We" is fine for context, but every <em>action</em> in this story should start with "I."</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q7 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">07</div>
        <div class="q-text">What do you know about our company?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Did you do five minutes of homework, or did you do real homework? The bar for this question is shockingly low — clear it by a mile.</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "Talabat is the leading online food and grocery delivery platform in MENA, part of Delivery Hero since 2015, operating across <strong>9 countries</strong> with <strong>~6.5 million active customers</strong> and <strong>68,000+ active partners</strong>. In Egypt specifically, Talabat leads the food delivery market with somewhere around <strong>945,000 weekly active users</strong>, and the q-commerce arm — Talabat Mart — competes directly with Rabbit, Breadfast, and Carrefour Now. The <strong>InstaShop acquisition that closed in early 2025</strong> meaningfully expanded the grocery and convenience footprint. What I find most interesting strategically is that around <strong>95% of food delivery orders in Egypt are still placed by phone</strong>, not via app — so the real growth ceiling here isn't market share against other apps, it's converting offline ordering to digital. Partnerships, especially with banks and telcos, are a natural lever for that conversion."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">The "95% offline" insight is the kind of thing that makes hiring managers lean forward. It signals she's thinking about the business, not just memorizing the About Us page.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q8 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">08</div>
        <div class="q-text">What is your ideal work environment?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Will you fit our culture? Talabat is fast-paced, MENA-region collaborative, performance-driven, slightly chaotic in the best way. They want signal that this energizes you, not drains you.</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "I do my best work in environments that are <strong>fast-moving but accountable</strong> — where decisions get made quickly with the data available, then revisited honestly when results come in. I also thrive when the team has a <strong>genuine commercial bias</strong>, where marketing isn't separated from the P&amp;L conversation. From what I've read about Talabat — the speed of category launches, the depth of the regional collaboration, the fact that the partnerships team sits inside the broader marketing strategy — that's the kind of environment where I tend to do my sharpest work. I also do well when there's <strong>real autonomy on the deal but a clear ritual for sharing back</strong> — weekly pipeline reviews, monthly partner reports — that combination of independence and visibility."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Avoid: "I love a calm environment" or "I just need clear instructions." Both signal poor fit for a partnerships role. The right energy is <em>structured ambition</em>.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q9 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">09</div>
        <div class="q-text">Describe your work ethic.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">How do you operate when no one is watching? Do you finish things? Do you escalate when needed, or do you sit on problems?</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Model answer structure</div>
        <div class="model-answer">
          "Three things define how I work. First, <strong>I close loops</strong> — if I owe someone something, internal or external, I send it before they have to ask. That sounds small, but in partnership work it's the difference between deals that move and deals that drift. Second, <strong>I work backwards from the outcome</strong> — every Monday I look at what I want to be true by Friday, then plan backwards. Third, <strong>I escalate early when I'm stuck, not late</strong> — I'd rather ask a manager for 15 minutes on Tuesday than miss a deadline on Friday because I was too proud to ask. The throughline is that I treat the work as something I'm accountable for, not just assigned to."
        </div>

        <div class="answer-label tip">Delivery notes</div>
        <p class="answer-text">This is one of the few questions where naming three things is fine — work ethic is naturally listy. End on the throughline sentence; it gives the answer a clean close.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ═════════════ 05 PARTNERSHIPS ═════════════ -->
  <section id="partnerships">
    <div class="section-num">§ 05</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Partnership-specific <em>questions</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">These are the questions most likely to surface in the Spark Hire round if it's tailored to the role — and almost certain to come up in the hiring manager interview. Each one is calibrated to the exact responsibilities listed in the JD.</p>

    <!-- Q10 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">10</div>
        <div class="q-text">Walk me through a partnership you built from scratch — from sourcing to launch to measurement.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Can she actually run a deal end-to-end, or has she only ever managed an account someone else closed? This is the single most important question of the entire process.</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Answer framework — five beats</div>
        <p class="answer-text">
          <strong>1. Sourcing —</strong> how did the opportunity come up? Inbound, outbound, warm intro, market mapping?<br>
          <strong>2. Qualification —</strong> what made you believe it was worth your time? What was the strategic fit?<br>
          <strong>3. Negotiation —</strong> what were the friction points? What did you trade?<br>
          <strong>4. Launch —</strong> who internally did you need to align? What almost broke?<br>
          <strong>5. Measurement —</strong> what was the KPI? What did you actually deliver? What did you learn for next time?
        </p>
        <div class="tags">
          <span class="tag">Ownership</span>
          <span class="tag">Negotiation</span>
          <span class="tag">Cross-functional</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q11 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">11</div>
        <div class="q-text">How do you decide whether a potential partnership is worth pursuing?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Do you have a real framework, or do you chase whatever inbound shows up? The good answer shows discipline.</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Answer framework</div>
        <p class="answer-text">A clean three-filter model: <strong>(1) Strategic fit</strong> — does it match a current brand or commercial priority? <strong>(2) Effort-to-impact ratio</strong> — what's the lift required versus the realistic outcome? <strong>(3) Optionality</strong> — does it open doors to a category, a flagship case study, or a longer-term relationship? If two of three are weak, decline politely and revisit in 6 months.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q12 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">12</div>
        <div class="q-text">Describe a time you had to negotiate against difficult terms and still close the deal.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Negotiation is the single most-named hard skill in the JD. They want a story with a real trade-off, not "we just talked it through."</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Strong-answer ingredients</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Name the specific friction (revenue share, exclusivity, marketing investment, timeline). Name the creative alternative you proposed. Name the outcome with a number. Bonus: name the relationship status afterwards — did the partner come back for round two?</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q13 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">13</div>
        <div class="q-text">A partner you manage is consistently underperforming on agreed deliverables. What's your playbook?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Answer framework</div>
        <p class="answer-text">
          <strong>1. Diagnose privately first</strong> — pull the data, identify whether it's a tracking issue, a market issue, or a partner-execution issue. Don't accuse without evidence.<br>
          <strong>2. Have a direct conversation</strong> — share the data, ask their interpretation, agree on a 30-day plan.<br>
          <strong>3. Document and escalate internally only if needed</strong> — let leadership know early but with a recommendation, not just a problem.<br>
          <strong>4. Decide</strong> — renegotiate, restructure, or sunset. The worst outcome is a zombie partnership consuming your time.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q14 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">14</div>
        <div class="q-text">How do you build and maintain your partner pipeline?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Answer framework</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Mention <strong>three sources</strong>: market mapping (top 20 brands per category, refreshed quarterly), warm network (referrals from existing partners, agencies, industry events), and inbound qualification. Then talk about <strong>stage-gating</strong>: how you move opportunities through Discover → Qualify → Propose → Negotiate → Close → Activate, ideally in a tracker you review weekly with your manager.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q15 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">15</div>
        <div class="q-text">Tell me about a time you had to work across Finance, Legal, and Marketing to ship a partnership.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">The JD explicitly names finance, legal, tech, and the regional team. They want to know she's worked in matrix orgs before and doesn't get blocked at the first redline.</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Strong-answer ingredients</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Name the deal. Name the friction (legal redlines on data sharing, finance margin guardrails, marketing brand-guideline conflicts). Show how you ran the coordination — single source of truth, regular sync, clear deadlines. End with the launch.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q16 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">16</div>
        <div class="q-text">How do you measure the success of a partnership beyond just revenue?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Answer framework</div>
        <p class="answer-text">A balanced scorecard: <strong>commercial</strong> (revenue, basket size, frequency), <strong>brand</strong> (impressions, share of voice, sentiment), <strong>customer</strong> (new users acquired, retention impact, NPS lift), and <strong>strategic</strong> (category exclusivity unlocked, doors opened to similar partners). The honest answer is that different partnerships should be judged on different mixes of these — a bank partnership might be 70% customer acquisition, an FMCG one might be 50% revenue and 50% media value.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q17 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">17</div>
        <div class="q-text">How do you stay close to your partners between campaigns?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Answer framework</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Mention a <strong>cadence</strong>: monthly check-in calls, a quarterly business review with data, an annual planning workshop. Mention <strong>warm touchpoints</strong>: sending them a relevant insight unprompted, intros to other relevant people inside Talabat, public credit on case studies. The signal is that you treat partners as relationships, not transactions.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q18 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">18</div>
        <div class="q-text">Tell me about a partnership that didn't work. What did you learn?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label purpose">What they're really asking</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Can you talk honestly about failure without either blaming or self-flagellating? Mature professionals own outcomes and extract learning.</p>

        <div class="answer-label">Strong-answer ingredients</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Pick a real one. Be specific about what went wrong (misaligned KPIs, wrong audience, wrong partner stage). Be specific about your share of the responsibility. End with a <em>changed behaviour</em>: "Since then, I always do X before signing."</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

