    </div>

    <div class="callout pro">
      <div class="callout-title">Likely partner categories to know</div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Banks:</strong> CIB, NBE, QNB Alahli, Banque Misr — for cashback, instalments, co-branded promos.</li>
        <li><strong>Fintech / wallets:</strong> Vodafone Cash, Instapay, Fawry, ValU — for payment-led offers.</li>
        <li><strong>FMCG:</strong> P&amp;G, Unilever, Nestlé, PepsiCo, Coca-Cola — for bundled promotions and category exclusives on Talabat Mart.</li>
        <li><strong>Telco:</strong> Vodafone, Orange, Etisalat (we) — bundled subscriptions, loyalty.</li>
        <li><strong>Entertainment:</strong> Anghami, Shahid, OSN+ — content trials with order bundles.</li>
        <li><strong>Real estate / lifestyle:</strong> Compounds and developers (SODIC, Palm Hills, Madinaty) for resident-perks deals.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═════════════ 07 CASES ═════════════ -->
  <section id="cases">
    <div class="section-num">§ 07</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Mini-cases &amp; <em>situational questions</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">These typically show up in rounds 3, 4, and 5 — but it's not unheard of for one to land in the Spark Hire round. Always start with a clarifying question or a stated assumption, then structure the answer.</p>

    <!-- Q19 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">19</div>
        <div class="q-text">If I asked you to launch a co-marketing partnership between Talabat and a major Egyptian bank in the next 90 days, how would you approach it?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Suggested structure</div>
        <p class="answer-text">
          <strong>Week 1–2:</strong> Define the goal (acquisition? frequency? AOV? brand?). Map the top 3 bank candidates based on cardholder base, demographic fit, and existing partnership posture (CIB, NBE, QNB Alahli). Pick one primary, one backup.<br>
          <strong>Week 3–4:</strong> Internal alignment — Finance on the margin envelope, Legal on data-sharing limits, Marketing on creative bandwidth, Tech on any redemption/voucher flow needed.<br>
          <strong>Week 5–6:</strong> Pitch the bank with a co-branded offer (e.g., 20% off Friday orders for cardholders, capped). Negotiate split of media spend.<br>
          <strong>Week 7–8:</strong> Build creative, set tracking, brief CX and ops teams.<br>
          <strong>Week 9–12:</strong> Launch in waves (one segment first), monitor daily, optimise mid-flight, deliver post-mortem with renewal recommendation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q20 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">20</div>
        <div class="q-text">A major FMCG partner wants to do a campaign that's great for their brand but mediocre for Talabat's customer experience. How do you handle it?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Suggested answer</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Don't refuse outright and don't accept outright. Reframe the conversation around the <strong>shared customer</strong>: "Here's what we'd need to change to make this work for the user — and here's why that'll actually drive better outcomes for your brand too." Propose <strong>two modified versions</strong> of the campaign. If they still won't budge, escalate internally with a clear recommendation rather than letting it linger.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q21 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">21</div>
        <div class="q-text">How would you build the case to convince leadership to invest in a partnership category we haven't entered before — say, real estate developers?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Suggested structure</div>
        <p class="answer-text">A one-page memo with: <strong>the customer insight</strong> (e.g., compound residents have higher AOV and frequency), <strong>the addressable opportunity</strong> (X compounds × Y residents × estimated incremental orders), <strong>three model deal structures</strong> (sponsored launch, resident perks, exclusive promo days), <strong>required investment</strong>, and <strong>a 90-day pilot</strong> with one or two named developers and a clear success threshold for scaling.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q22 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">22</div>
        <div class="q-text">A competitor (say, Rabbit or Keeta) just announced a major partnership in a category we were targeting. What do you do?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Suggested answer</div>
        <p class="answer-text">Don't panic-react. Three steps: <strong>(1) Read the deal carefully</strong> — exclusivity terms, duration, financial commitment. Often the announcement is bigger than the reality. <strong>(2) Reassess your own pipeline</strong> — is your alternative partner in that category still viable? Sometimes the competitor took the second-best option. <strong>(3) If genuinely blocked, pivot</strong> — propose a different angle in the same category, or escalate strategically about whether to match or differentiate.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Q23 -->
    <article class="q-card">
      <div class="q-header">
        <div class="q-number">23</div>
        <div class="q-text">Your partnerships pipeline is empty heading into Q2. What are the first three things you do?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="q-body">
        <div class="answer-label">Suggested answer</div>
        <p class="answer-text"><strong>1.</strong> Reactivate the dormant list — every partner we've ever talked to who said "maybe next quarter." Most of them have rolled into a new fiscal year and have fresh budget.<br><strong>2.</strong> Run a quick market map of the top 20 brands per category we haven't approached, prioritized by quarterly relevance (e.g., FMCG for Ramadan, banks for back-to-school).<br><strong>3.</strong> Activate the internal network — ask the broader marketing team, regional team, and senior leadership who they could intro me to in the next two weeks.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <!-- ═════════════ 08 QUESTIONS TO ASK ═════════════ -->
  <section id="ask">
    <div class="section-num">§ 08</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Questions to <em>ask them</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">In the live rounds. Spark Hire usually doesn't include a "do you have questions" prompt, but if it does — or in the HR screen and hiring manager round — these are the ones that demonstrate seniority without sounding rehearsed.</p>

    <div class="snapshot-grid">
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>About the role</h3>
        <p>"What does success look like in this role at the 6-month and 12-month mark?"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>About the team</h3>
        <p>"Who would I work most closely with day-to-day across marketing, commercial, and the regional team?"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>About the pipeline</h3>
        <p>"What does the current partnership portfolio look like — which categories are mature, and which are still being built?"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>About strategy</h3>
        <p>"How is the partnerships team positioned relative to growth, brand, and commercial — who owns the P&amp;L and how are trade-offs made?"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>About the competition</h3>
        <p>"With Keeta's expected entry and the q-commerce competition heating up, how is partnerships strategy adapting?"</p>
      </div>
      <div class="snapshot-card">
        <h3>About growth</h3>
        <p>"This role is listed as Temporary — what does the path to permanent or to expanded scope typically look like?"</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠ Don't ask in the early rounds</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Salary, vacation, working hours, benefits, or remote flexibility — save those for the offer stage.</li>
        <li>Anything Google could answer in 10 seconds ("How many countries does Talabat operate in?").</li>
        <li>"Do you have any concerns about my candidacy?" — it's become a cliché and puts the interviewer on the spot.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═════════════ 09 SOAR ═════════════ -->
  <section id="toolkit">
    <div class="section-num">§ 09</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">The SOAR <em>answer toolkit</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">For any behavioral question ("Tell me about a time when…"), the SOAR framework keeps answers structured, complete, and roughly the right length. It's a more action-oriented variant of STAR. Use it on every behavioral question, even ones not in this guide.</p>

    <div class="soar-grid">
      <div class="soar-card">
        <div class="soar-letter">S</div>
        <h5>Situation</h5>
        <p>Set the scene in 1–2 sentences. Name the company, the role, the timeframe, what was at stake. Don't over-context.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="soar-card">
        <div class="soar-letter">O</div>
        <h5>Obstacle</h5>
        <p>What was hard? Why didn't this just resolve itself? The obstacle is what makes the story worth telling — name it explicitly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="soar-card">
        <div class="soar-letter">A</div>
        <h5>Actions</h5>
        <p>What did <em>you</em> do? Every sentence here should start with "I." Two or three specific actions, in sequence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="soar-card">
        <div class="soar-letter">R</div>
        <h5>Result</h5>
        <p>What happened? A number is best. A learning is acceptable. A vague "it went well" is not.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout pro">
      <div class="callout-title">The 90-second rule</div>
      <p>Behavioral answers on Spark Hire should land between 60 and 90 seconds. That works out to roughly: <strong>10 sec Situation · 15 sec Obstacle · 45 sec Actions · 20 sec Result</strong>. The Actions section gets the most airtime because that's the part the reviewer cares about most.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═════════════ 10 TRAPS ═════════════ -->
  <section id="traps">
    <div class="section-num">§ 10</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Red-flag traps <em>to avoid</em></h2>
    <p class="section-intro">A short list of things candidates routinely do that quietly tank otherwise strong applications.</p>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠ Content traps</div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Talking only about marketing, not commercial outcomes.</strong> Every partnership story should end with a number — revenue, customer count, retention, basket lift.</li>
        <li><strong>Using "we" instead of "I" for actions.</strong> The interviewer can't credit work to her if it's narrated as team work. "We" for context, "I" for actions.</li>
        <li><strong>Being vague about partners.</strong> Name names, even if the partner is small. Concrete &gt; abstract.</li>
        <li><strong>Trash-talking previous employers.</strong> Even if true, this is the single fastest way to lose a recruiter. Frame everything as a learning, not a complaint.</li>
        <li><strong>Pretending to know something she doesn't.</strong> "I haven't worked directly with banks, but here's how I'd approach it…" is a stronger answer than bluffing.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout warn">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠ Delivery traps on Spark Hire specifically</div>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reading from a script.</strong> Bullet points only. Scripts make the eyes go flat and the voice go monotone.</li>
        <li><strong>Burning all retakes early.</strong> Use the first take as the real one. Retakes are insurance, not perfectionism.</li>
        <li><strong>Going way over time on every question.</strong> Better to finish at 75% than to run out mid-sentence at 100%.</li>
        <li><strong>Filming in poor light or with a noisy background.</strong> The recruiter doesn't care, but the AI screening tools do — and so does her own confidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Skipping the practice video.</strong> It's free. Always take it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout tip">
      <div class="callout-title">✦ The one thing that beats everything else</div>
      <p>Specificity. The candidate who says "I negotiated a renewal with CIB at 70% better terms than the previous year by restructuring the cashback rules" beats the candidate who says "I have strong negotiation skills" every single time, even if they're the same person. Names, numbers, dates. Always.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

